Lemma 6.2,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"∀ t ∈ [0 , T ] , ‖ µ 1 ( t ) -µ 2 ( t ) ‖ 1 ≤ ‖ µ 1 (0) -µ 2 (0) ‖ 1 +2 ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ ∫ t 0 ‖ I 1 ( τ ) -I 2 ( τ ) ‖ ∞ dτ","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":464.3178152484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.31200000000004,"r":514.6281568,"t":490.7905929484375},"charspan":[0,119],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Then, since µ 1 (0) = µ 2 (0) = n ini , we obtain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":440.6068152484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.57900000000006,"r":292.8295788800001,"t":452.30269944843747},"charspan":[0,49],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/13","text":"Then, since µ 1 (0) = µ 2 (0) = n ini , we obtain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"∀ t ∈ [0 , T ] , ‖ µ 1 ( t ) -µ 2 ( t ) ‖ 1 ≤ 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ ‖ I 1 -I 2 ‖ ∞ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":414.2058152484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":153.56000000000006,"r":441.7158144000001,"t":425.90169944843745},"charspan":[0,78],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/14","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"And so","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":388.8247058484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.56700000000012,"r":107.71713376000014,"t":399.50069944843744},"charspan":[0,6],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/15","text":"And so"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"‖ µ 1 -µ 2 ‖ X ≤ 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ ‖ I 1 -I 2 ‖ ∞ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":373.35881524843745,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":205.3620000000001,"r":389.9138144000002,"t":385.0546994484374},"charspan":[0,49],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/16","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Finally, if n 1 , n 2 ∈ X , and µ ( n 1 ) , µ ( n 2 ) ∈ X are the two solutions associated to the activity functions I 1 = F ( n 1 ) and I 2 = F ( n 2 ) and with µ 1 (0) = µ 2 (0) = n ini , then,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":337.99181524843755,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":523.2713291775996,"t":364.1336994484375},"charspan":[0,195],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/17","text":"Finally, if n 1 , n 2 ∈ X , and µ ( n 1 ) , µ ( n 2 ) ∈ X are the two solutions associated to the activity functions I 1 = F ( n 1 ) and I 2 = F ( n 2 ) and with µ 1 (0) = µ 2 (0) = n ini , then,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"‖ µ ( n 1 ) -µ ( n 2 ) ‖ X ≤ 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ ‖ I 1 -I 2 ‖ ∞ . ≤ 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ r M ‖ n 1 -n 2 ‖ X","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":294.5048152484376,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":182.13400000000013,"r":412.02107039000015,"t":323.63569944843744},"charspan":[0,98],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/18","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Now, if we take T > 0 such that 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ r M < 1, then the function n ↦→ µ ( n ) is a contraction on X and therefore, according to Picard Fixed point Theorem, there exist a unique function on X such that µ ( n ) = n . This function is the solution of (1) on [0 , T ].","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":240.3817058484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":525.2257778175994,"t":279.9496994484375},"charspan":[0,274],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/19","text":"Now, if we take T > 0 such that 2 T ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ r M < 1, then the function n ↦→ µ ( n ) is a contraction on X and therefore, according to Picard Fixed point Theorem, there exist a unique function on X such that µ ( n ) = n . This function is the solution of (1) on [0 , T ]."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Finally, we use the same argument on every interval [ kT, ( k +1) T ], with k ∈ N to prove that the solution n is uniquely and well-defined on R + .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":197.0447058484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":523.2759222016001,"t":222.1666994484375},"charspan":[0,148],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/20","text":"Finally, we use the same argument on every interval [ kT, ( k +1) T ], with k ∈ N to prove that the solution n is uniquely and well-defined on R + ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Lemma 6.2. Let µ 1 and µ 2 be the solution of (26) with respectively r 1 ( t, x ) := r ( x, I 1 ( t )) and r 2 ( t, x ) = r ( x, I 2 ( t )) , µ 1 (0) = µ 0 1 and µ 2 (0) = µ 0 2 . Then,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":156.0078152484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.103,"r":524.6459787136,"t":183.3116994484376},"charspan":[0,185],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/21","text":"Lemma 6.2. Let µ 1 and µ 2 be the solution of (26) with respectively r 1 ( t, x ) := r ( x, I 1 ( t )) and r 2 ( t, x ) = r ( x, I 2 ( t )) , µ 1 (0) = µ 0 1 and µ 2 (0) = µ 0 2 . Then,"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"∀ t ≥ 0 , ‖ µ 1 ( t ) -µ 2 ( t ) ‖ 1 ≤ ‖ µ 0 1 -µ 0 2 ‖ 1 +2 ‖ ∂ I r ‖ ∞ ∫ t 0 ‖ I 1 ( τ ) -I 2 ( τ ) ‖ ∞ dτ.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":118.6378152484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":114.162,"r":481.1104176000001,"t":145.11059294843744},"charspan":[0,109],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/22","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"22","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":39.792705848437436,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":291.7850000000001,"r":303.4915318400001,"t":50.46869944843752},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/23","text":"22"}],"version":"1.10.0"}